<section class="evidence-band" aria-labelledby="cadran-what-heading">
  <div class="section-inner band-grid">
    <div>
      <p class="section-label">Object record / CDN-01</p>
      <h2 id="cadran-what-heading">Twelve positions that are operating modes, not hours.</h2>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="stamp-row" aria-label="Le Cadran status">
        <span class="stamp">Live in the terminal</span>
        <span class="stamp stamp-muted">Prototype 0</span>
        <span class="stamp stamp-muted">Case not built</span>
      </p>
      <p class="entry-note">ATLAS runs as one assistant in many modes. Le Conseil names those modes (the skeptic, the archivist, the bloodhound, the smith and the rest) and decides which of them answer a given message. Le Cadran is the dial that shows the outcome: each lit position is a member who actually convened, the hands point to who answered and who took precedence, and anything the system can't measure yet is drawn and labelled <strong>undriven</strong>.</p>
      <p class="entry-note">It's laid out as a diver's chronograph because every part of that movement carries a real constraint. The bezel turns one way only, which is how Le Sauvegarder works. The crown screws down, so nothing changes by accident. A guarded pusher has to be armed before Le Fripon can be used, and the arming expires.</p>
      <p class="action-row"><a href="/projects/atlas/le-cadran/live.html">Open the live instrument</a></p>
      <p class="action-row"><a href="/projects/atlas/le-cadran/sheet.html">Open the full object record (Plates 1–4)</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="source-band" aria-labelledby="cadran-rule-heading">
  <div class="section-inner band-grid">
    <div>
      <p class="section-label">The honesty constraint</p>
      <h2 id="cadran-rule-heading">The display never shows a state the router did not produce.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="callout">
      <h3>In software it is a discipline.</h3>
      <p>Every lit element traces back to a field in a routing trace emitted by Le Rouage, the routing train. There's no demo loop, idle animation or startup sweep that lights positions for effect. Dark positions are genuinely dark: the gate did not fire.</p>
      <h3>In hardware it is a wiring constraint.</h3>
      <p>The case's LEDs are driven by that same trace, so the physical object can't be finished before the software behind it is. An instrument that displays invented state is a prop.</p>
      <h3>Undriven is a reading, not a gap.</h3>
      <p>The registers (Signal, Noise, Gain), the up-and-down reserve (Le Barillet), the calendar (Le Continuant) and the retrograde arc are drawn on the face and marked undriven. They'll light when something real drives them, and not before.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="evidence-band" aria-labelledby="cadran-live-heading">
  <div class="section-inner band-grid">
    <div>
      <p class="section-label fi-caption-box"><span class="fi-prompt-marker" aria-hidden="true">&gt;</span>LIVE / IN THE BROWSER</p>
      <h2 id="cadran-live-heading">Type something. The council routes it.</h2>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="entry-note">The live instrument runs the same routing train in your browser: <code>train.js</code>, held to the Python <code>rouage.py</code> by a conformance suite. Type a message and the dial and the terminal face redraw from the trace it produces. Matching is by named invocation, so a route needs its verb. Try <code>run Publish</code>, not "publish". The page also takes a proposed member and a reason, standing in for the assistant's own call.</p>
      <p class="entry-note">Nothing on it is sent anywhere. It's a static page with no network requests, rendered by ATLAS <code>rouage/live.py</code> and published here unchanged.</p>
      <p class="action-row"><a href="/projects/atlas/le-cadran/live.html">Open Le Cadran · Live</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

