<section class="evidence-band" aria-labelledby="cadran-sequence-heading">
  <div class="section-inner band-grid">
    <div>
      <p class="section-label">Sequence / 2026</p>
      <h2 id="cadran-sequence-heading">From a council to a dial to a face in the terminal.</h2>
    </div>
    <ol class="progression-list">
      <li class="complete">
        <span>08.16</span>
        <h3>Inception</h3>
        <p>The council is rebuilt as a diver's movement, and the case is specified the same night, honesty constraint included. A marine chronometer was drafted first and sent back: it has neither a rotating bezel nor a crown.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="complete">
        <span>08.26</span>
        <h3>The going train</h3>
        <p>Le Rouage, the router, lands with the first SVG dial (<a href="https://github.com/SCADUMEN/ATLAS/pull/2">#2</a>). Prototype 0 is started: the layout under test, honest by construction.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="complete">
        <span>08.29</span>
        <h3>The dial catches a bug</h3>
        <p>Position 12 lit, and the reason engraved on the dial was the exact sentence written to keep him dark. The negative gate was fixed (<a href="https://github.com/SCADUMEN/ATLAS/pull/24">#24</a>).</p>
      </li>
      <li class="complete">
        <span>10.02</span>
        <h3>A round face in the terminal</h3>
        <p>The dial is redrawn in character cells; the hands were chosen by L'Opérateur from four candidates (<a href="https://github.com/SCADUMEN/ATLAS/pull/50">#50</a>), then split into a face for glancing and a table for reading (<a href="https://github.com/SCADUMEN/ATLAS/pull/52">#52</a>).</p>
      </li>
      <li class="complete">
        <span>10.09</span>
        <h3>Every prompt</h3>
        <p>The train runs on every message through a prompt hook (<a href="https://github.com/SCADUMEN/ATLAS/pull/56">#56</a>), and a stop hook is added to draw the face after the turn settles (<a href="https://github.com/SCADUMEN/ATLAS/pull/59">#59</a>). Its display in a live session is not yet verified.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="complete">
        <span>10.25</span>
        <h3>Live in the browser</h3>
        <p>The live page draws the terminal face and takes a proposal (<a href="https://github.com/SCADUMEN/ATLAS/pull/62">#62</a>). This sub-dossier makes the sheet and the instrument public.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="active">
        <span>Next</span>
        <h3>Prototype 1 / .30 cal</h3>
        <p>The case: specified, not built.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="source-band" aria-labelledby="cadran-case-heading">
  <div class="section-inner band-grid">
    <div>
      <p class="section-label">Le Boîtier / the case</p>
      <h2 id="cadran-case-heading">Prototype 1 goes into a .30 cal ammo can, turned on its side.</h2>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="stamp-row" aria-label="Case status">
        <span class="stamp">Specified</span>
        <span class="stamp stamp-muted">Not built</span>
      </p>
      <p class="entry-note">An M19A1 can laid on its side carries a dial of six to six and a half inches. Behind diffused acrylic batons sit addressable RGB LEDs, one per position, with the hands on a geared stepper and a single-board computer in the three-inch depth. The can's existing latch and hinge become the caseback, so the instrument opens the way its doctrine says a case opens. Pusher, crown and pusher come through the side under a fabricated guard.</p>
      <p class="entry-note">Prototype 0 comes first: a four-inch round panel showing the existing interface at real size, the cheapest way to find out that a marker is unreadable at arm's length.</p>
      <div class="callout">
        <h3>What the case does not do.</h3>
        <p>It doesn't run the council, store L'Archive, or decide anything. The instrument is read. L'Opérateur decides.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

